new file mode 100644
index 00000000..c13e5159
--- /dev/null
+++ b/website/docs/developer-guide/plugins/memory-owner-v1.md
@@ -0,0 +1,110 @@
+# Experimental memory ownership v1 (local rebuild, not upstream)
+
+A plugin session RPC can call `hermes_cli.plugin_memory.memory_owner(version=1)`.
+The host supplies its authenticated actor, profile authority and canonical conversation.
+Optional `alias` selects a validated lineage member; it cannot select another owner.
+The frozen result contains version, canonical/physical session ids, aliases, native profile
+and principal ids, runtime revision, captured authority epoch, and optional stored project selection.
+Keep native profile ids internal: they can contain local absolute paths.
+
+## Zero-write local ingress
+
+Register `ctx.register_rpc('memory.owner', handler, memory_scope='local_read')`.
+This requires a canonical local receipt and session:read, validates ownership off-loop,
+and does not mount/restore sessions, adopt compression, or repair routes. Calls with
+untracked compression fail closed. No cwd/sidebar/project inference is performed.
+The handler still runs trusted plugin code: this is not a sandbox preventing arbitrary
+file access or all possible side effects. The memory publication helper is disabled here.
+
+Existing registrations without memory_scope retain their previous session:control gate,
+which MAY restore/adopt/update native state before a handler runs. This behavior was not
+removed. No promise of zero-write behavior applies to those ordinary RPCs.
+
+Context ends when its handler returns, including escaped copied contexts. Unbound jobs,
+sessionless calls and unsupported session kinds refuse; snapshots are not permission tokens.
+
+## Bounded external publication
+
+Inside an ordinary authenticated control RPC, obtain a fresh snapshot, then call
+`publish_memory(snapshot, synchronous_callback)`. The helper validates scope, snapshot,
+native runtime epoch, current ownership/lineage/revision under BEGIN IMMEDIATE and the
+native store lock. It invokes the callback at most once per call, with NO callback retries.
+The callback must be short and synchronous, must not reenter SessionDB, await, delegate,
+or wait for a job that needs the native writer lock. Finish expensive work beforehand.
+
+The SQLite writer exclusion serializes cooperative reset/delete/rebind/epoch writes
+against this callback. It does not create a distributed transaction with the memory
+store. Callback failure or failed postchecks means `memory_publication_uncertain`:
+inspect the destination before retrying, even when the host reports failure.
+There is no durable request-id deduplication; repeating the public call can repeat work.
+
+In-memory retirement/context expiry and uncoordinated replacement of database files cannot
+be atomically excluded by SQLite; checks before/after catch known invalidation, but external
+writes already performed cannot be undone. This is NOT crash-safe exactly-once publication.
+A production organizer still needs destination idempotency/receipts and reconciliation.
+
+Full organizer preview/save/execute, capture wiring, Windows
+qualification and native UI restoration remain separate work.
+
+A later handler exception or JSON serialization failure after successful publication can
+produce generic `plugin_rpc_failed`, not the helper's uncertainty error. Never infer no
+external effect from ANY failed RPC reply. Publication remains in-process only. Read-only hosted compatibility is described below.
+
+## Optional process-isolated reads (recovery r4)
+
+Official Hermes defaults to `plugins.isolation: in_process`. Under opt-in `host`,
+the public owner read forwards through the existing request-origin channel into the
+parent's authenticated context. Parent bindings check host generation, plugin/load,
+profile and active invocation; identity fields are not accepted from child payloads.
+Stale/unbound/background invocations refuse. Publication callbacks remain unsupported
+across this boundary; no callback is serialized or executed remotely by this API.
+
+The shared per-profile plugin host remains trusted code, not a malicious-code sandbox.
+Plugins in the same process can inspect shared memory; this bridge does not claim to
+isolate one malicious plugin from another. Its guards prevent accidental context/load
+confusion and refuse calls without the existing parent-authorized invocation.
+
+ELM probes memory_capabilities before registering the new diagnostic, so unpatched
+hosts do not receive an unsupported memory_scope keyword. This preserves pre-existing
+registration behavior, not a claim that all older ELM workspace APIs work on stock Hermes.
+
+## Explicit project selection (recovery r5)
+
+In a control RPC, `bind_memory_project(snapshot, exact_project_id)` explicitly selects a
+native project, or `None` clears selection. This is never inferred from cwd, folder, slug
+or active-project UI. It uses snapshot compare-and-swap; stale revisions refuse.
+A project ID grants no cross-principal sharing: memory namespaces MUST retain profile,
+principal and project ID. Native projects have no per-principal ACL.
+
+The owner snapshot now includes stored `project_id`, `project_revision` and
+`project_fingerprint`. It deliberately remains readable after catalog deletion/archive
+so selection can be cleared. **This snapshot is not current project-read authorization.**
+No project-wide retrieval path is implemented by this checkpoint.
+
+Binding and bound publication acquire the existing projects.db writer lock BEFORE the
+state.db writer lock. The exact active catalog row is fingerprinted and revalidated;
+archive/delete/rename refuse publication before the callback. Unbinding uses state only,
+so it works when the catalog is missing. Native catalog data/schema is not modified.
+Only the state selection commits: this is not a cross-database atomic commit protocol.
+
+Every row-field change conservatively invalidates the fingerprint until explicit rebind.
+Archive then restore of the same row re-enables current eligibility, not permanent
+historical revocation. The catalog has no lifecycle generation suitable for stronger
+ABA guarantees. Manual database replacement/forged identical rows remain outside scope.
+
+Before/after-commit authority and context checks detect known retirement. A commit error
+or postcommit invalidation reports `memory_binding_uncertain`; read current selection
+before retrying. No automatic retry occurs. Hosted selection mutation remains unsupported.
+Native UI wiring, orphan cleanup/rekey migration and full project retrieval authorization
+remain unimplemented and must not be advertised as complete integration.
+
+## Bounded record reads (recovery r7)
+
+`read_memory(snapshot, reader)` permits a read-capability RPC, including local_read scope.
+The trusted callback must be short, synchronous and side-effect-free. Catalog-before-state
+writer exclusion stabilizes current ownership/selection; both host transactions roll back.
+Results are copied into inert built-in data inside the fence (no lazy/custom return objects),
+then final host/context/catalog checks must pass before disclosure. No callback retry.
+This authenticates an owner snapshot, NOT record-level consent. ELM must independently enforce
+private scope, pending/approved state, sensitive filtering and future project policies.
+Arbitrary callbacks remain in-process only. The new private-chat lane never enables project reads.
